WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040
More than 8,200 U.S. flights were canceled Friday, straining passengers nationwide. This

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:12.660
is Omni View Top World News, episode 53. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. After

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:17.120
that, the fight over what actually overloaded the radios, then a restoration Microsoft

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.100
says is finished. In the United States, the Federal Aviation Administration ordered a

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:27.380
rare nationwide ground stop on Friday afternoon. The New York Times reported, once outdated

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:32.260
air traffic communications gear overloaded with data and started to fail. Transportation

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:37.060
Secretary Sean Duffy, speaking at the White House that night, said radar and the rest of

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:42.100
the communications began to falter at 336 in the afternoon, when old routers took on more

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.480
data than they could carry. The breaks were not everywhere, he said. They were wide enough

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.240
that officials put a moratorium on takeoffs across the country, in order he called unusual.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:57.200
France 24 put a count on the cancelled day. More than 8,200 flights did not operate.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.680
Above 31% of everything scheduled in the United States. The whole ran nearly five hours.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:07.300
France 24 called it the longest nationwide ground stop in the country since September

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:13.660
11, 2001. Officials blamed aging communications equipment. The same report said investigators

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:18.820
had no evidence of a cyber attack. Airspace is open again. Flying has started back up.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.120
What the stop left behind is still moving.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.880
The time said thousands of passengers were stranded, and delays spread so widely that

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.420
airlines and airports were still trying to climb out of them.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.860
The paper tied the afternoon to weaknesses officials already treat as lasting in the

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.380
old U.S. air traffic network.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:39.320
And to a money fight, administration officials have asked lawmakers for tens of billions of

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.240
dollars more, so system-wide upgrades can move faster.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:43.780
That ask is a request.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.360
It is not money-voted.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.600
It is not new hardware already in place.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.740
Today's articles name no vote and no date for one.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.600
Duffy and Mark Wayne Mullin, whom the Times identified as the Homeland Security Secretary,

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.380
stood together and split the cause.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:02.700
Duffy insisted the fault was antiquated legacy communications equipment, not recent upgrades.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.700
Mullin said his side had seen zero evidence of a cyber attack, and he suggested that new

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:11.900
equipment and new technology from Duffy's department, laid on to the old infrastructure,

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.280
was what overwhelmed it.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.100
France 24's blame on outdated gear sits next to that split.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.300
Neither man said every sight failed.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.840
Both described a failure broad enough to freeze departure's nationwide.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.560
336 is when Duffy said the faltering began, not when the order ended.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.400
Nearly five hours is how long France 24 said the stop lasted.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.600
That window is a Friday afternoon and evening, when a national freeze hits whatever departures

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:37.400
are still on the board.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:41.780
If you were booked on one of those cancelled flights, the change was a departure that never

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:47.940
left, then a restart into a network already out of place. For a carrier, more than 8,200

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:52.640
cancellations in a single country on a single day is a national schedule broken open.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.220
Aircraft, crews, and connecting passengers in the wrong cities when the hold came off.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.880
The Times' unfinished recovery is the part that outlasts the stop.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.560
Takeoffs can be allowed again while later banks of flights are still late.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.760
France 24 can be right that flying has resumed, and the Times can be right that the timetable

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.900
is not whole. The share above 31 percent is a share of the U.S. schedule, not a head count.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.100
The Times' people figure is thousands stranded. The articles never turn those two measures into

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:25.560
one total of travelers turned away. What they fix is the order's reach, nationwide, unusual,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.980
and long enough for France 24 to set it against the ground stop of September 11, 2001.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.980
Past those hours, the argument is about the equipment the country flies on.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.600
The Times linked the stop to vulnerabilities officials call persistent,

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.100
and to that demand for tens of billions more.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.480
Duffy's version spends the money on replacing legacy routers

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.820
and communications gear he says failed by themselves.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.780
Mullins version spends it against a different risk.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.360
New technology piled on old infrastructure

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.100
until the old layer gives way.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.460
Nothing published from either man's remarks

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:58.540
is a joint finding that picks one account.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.040
What both put on the record is no evidence

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.040
in their telling of a cyber attack

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.060
and a failure series enough to stop take-offs everywhere.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.460
The step already taken is the lift.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:09.580
Flights are moving.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.180
The step still asked for is the funding demand.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:13.880
Still a demand on lawmakers.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.320
Still without a sum approved in these reports.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:17.740
Still without a date.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.980
Until Congress acts or declines,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.160
the network that failed on Friday afternoon

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.800
is the network airlines are using to rebuild Friday.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:26.920
People who were meant to fly did not.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.820
Crews and aircraft are still being put back in order.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.580
The officials in charge disagree in public.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.800
On whether the failure was the old equipment

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.500
or the new gear laid on top of it.

00:04:37.441 --> 00:04:39.681
The case on both sides is about that mechanism

00:04:39.681 --> 00:04:41.881
and about what tens of billions of dollars

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:43.361
in faster upgrades would buy.

00:04:43.861 --> 00:04:45.661
The stop itself is not the dispute.

00:04:46.201 --> 00:04:47.661
The Federal Aviation Administration

00:04:47.661 --> 00:04:49.741
ordered it after communications equipment

00:04:49.741 --> 00:04:51.781
overloaded with data and began to fail.

00:04:52.401 --> 00:04:54.301
Administration officials have asked Congress

00:04:54.301 --> 00:04:56.041
for tens of billions of dollars more.

00:04:56.581 --> 00:04:58.901
The operational next step is already written down.

00:04:59.421 --> 00:05:01.361
Airspace open, flights resumed,

00:05:01.741 --> 00:05:03.921
airlines and airports still struggling to recover.

00:05:03.921 --> 00:05:09.161
Duffy's case, given at the White House on Friday night, is that old routers overloaded

00:05:09.161 --> 00:05:14.521
at 336, that the fault is antiquated legacy equipment, and that recent upgrades did not

00:05:14.521 --> 00:05:15.021
cause it.

00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:19.941
On that telling, the reason to spend is replacement of gear that has already forced a nationwide

00:05:19.941 --> 00:05:20.661
moratorium.

00:05:21.181 --> 00:05:26.781
France 24's length, the longest such stop in the country since September 11, 2001, and

00:05:26.781 --> 00:05:32.661
its count, more than 8,200 flights, over 31 percent of the U.S. schedule, are the scale

00:05:32.661 --> 00:05:37.961
he can point to. He also said the failures were not universal, only pervasive enough to stop

00:05:37.961 --> 00:05:43.281
takeoffs everywhere. His strongest reason in his own terms is the overloaded old routers,

00:05:43.641 --> 00:05:48.241
and his rejection of recent upgrades as the cause. Mullins' case, made beside him,

00:05:48.441 --> 00:05:53.281
is the zero evidence of a cyberattack, and the suggestion that new equipment and new technology

00:05:53.281 --> 00:05:57.841
Duffy's department added to the old infrastructure did the overwhelming. On that telling,

00:05:57.841 --> 00:05:59.541
age is not the only risk.

00:05:59.801 --> 00:06:02.361
The risk is how new systems are laid onto old ones.

00:06:02.741 --> 00:06:06.701
A large new appropriation, spent the same way, could repeat the overload.

00:06:07.161 --> 00:06:09.581
His strongest reason is the one he offered that night.

00:06:10.021 --> 00:06:12.521
Added technology, not a hostile intrusion.

00:06:13.081 --> 00:06:17.581
France 24's report that officials blamed outdated equipment and that there was no evidence

00:06:17.581 --> 00:06:22.081
of a cyber attack, lines up with Duffy on age and with both men on the cyber question.

00:06:22.561 --> 00:06:26.481
It does not pick between Duffy's rejection of recent upgrades and Mullin's suggestion

00:06:26.481 --> 00:06:28.421
that added technology was the overload.

00:06:28.881 --> 00:06:31.141
What sits on the record is two official accounts

00:06:31.141 --> 00:06:33.621
of one Friday afternoon, a canceled share

00:06:33.621 --> 00:06:37.601
of the U.S. schedule above 31%, a stop of nearly five hours,

00:06:38.041 --> 00:06:39.661
and a funding demand the Times put it

00:06:39.661 --> 00:06:42.321
tens of billions of dollars more, still unvoted

00:06:42.321 --> 00:06:42.961
in these reports.

00:06:44.213 --> 00:06:46.653
A sign of progress is Microsoft's own declaration,

00:06:47.253 --> 00:06:49.633
reported by the Guardian, that on Saturday morning,

00:06:49.813 --> 00:06:51.953
it had restored service to the remaining customers

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:54.093
and regions hit by one of the worst information

00:06:54.093 --> 00:06:55.793
technology failures in years.

00:06:55.793 --> 00:07:01.473
On its Microsoft 365 status page, the company said it had finished the work of addressing

00:07:01.473 --> 00:07:05.613
the underlying issue and had brought service back for the remaining impacted customers

00:07:05.613 --> 00:07:06.193
and regions.

00:07:06.793 --> 00:07:11.213
The Guardian said Microsoft described the service as fully restored after a massive outage

00:07:11.213 --> 00:07:15.173
that had disrupted governments, businesses, and public services around the world.

00:07:15.653 --> 00:07:21.213
Microsoft said the outage had reached governments, courts, healthcare services, businesses, airlines

00:07:21.213 --> 00:07:22.833
and banks in many places.

00:07:23.253 --> 00:07:25.153
Japan, Singapore, and Australia

00:07:25.153 --> 00:07:27.393
each reported that the hours long episode

00:07:27.393 --> 00:07:29.033
had affected some of their operations.

00:07:29.673 --> 00:07:30.593
In the United Kingdom,

00:07:31.093 --> 00:07:32.813
hospital staff went to paper records

00:07:32.813 --> 00:07:35.173
and courtrooms went looking for fax machines.

00:07:35.733 --> 00:07:38.613
Users reported interruptions to Microsoft 365,

00:07:39.373 --> 00:07:40.833
including Outlook, Teams,

00:07:41.073 --> 00:07:43.513
and the Artificial Intelligence Assistant Co-Pilot.

00:07:43.993 --> 00:07:46.413
Down Detector said it took outage reports from users

00:07:46.413 --> 00:07:48.373
in more than 70 countries and territories

00:07:48.373 --> 00:07:50.713
and from 74 countries and territories

00:07:50.713 --> 00:07:53.133
within an hour after the problems first showed up.

00:07:53.653 --> 00:07:57.013
Microsoft said on its website that more than 430 million people

00:07:57.013 --> 00:07:59.393
use Microsoft 365 apps.

00:07:59.733 --> 00:08:02.753
It said in January that it had more than 450 million

00:08:02.753 --> 00:08:04.953
paid commercial subscriptions to those services.

00:08:05.533 --> 00:08:08.113
The Guardian treated those counts as Microsoft's measure

00:08:08.113 --> 00:08:09.693
of the base the outage could reach,

00:08:09.933 --> 00:08:12.393
not as a settled total of people actually cut off.

00:08:12.833 --> 00:08:15.013
Problems started appearing around mid-day Thursday

00:08:15.013 --> 00:08:17.453
in the United States and at six in the evening

00:08:17.453 --> 00:08:18.393
in the United Kingdom.

00:08:18.393 --> 00:08:21.373
The Washington-based company apologized on Friday.

00:08:21.773 --> 00:08:23.873
It said the outage was not related to cyber,

00:08:24.213 --> 00:08:26.513
and not related to artificial intelligence agents.

00:08:26.953 --> 00:08:29.113
It said the incident was introduced by an issue

00:08:29.113 --> 00:08:31.213
during an earlier network capacity upgrade

00:08:31.213 --> 00:08:33.533
that surfaced after a recent network change.

00:08:34.073 --> 00:08:35.853
The measured change is that restoration.

00:08:36.293 --> 00:08:38.233
A failure Microsoft itself ranked among

00:08:38.233 --> 00:08:40.813
the worst information technology failures in years,

00:08:41.253 --> 00:08:42.973
declared over for the remaining customers

00:08:42.973 --> 00:08:44.513
and regions on Saturday morning.

00:08:45.033 --> 00:08:46.533
The Guardian called the disruptions

00:08:46.533 --> 00:08:51.273
the latest case of cascading trouble when so many communications and technology platforms

00:08:51.273 --> 00:08:55.793
depend on one provider and said the outage fed worries in the United Kingdom about leaning

00:08:55.793 --> 00:09:00.513
on a small number of technology companies. Tim Clement-Jones, a former chair of the House

00:09:00.513 --> 00:09:05.313
of Lords Artificial Intelligence Select Committee, called the outages of vital government services

00:09:05.313 --> 00:09:10.433
and I told you so moment, on Britain's dependence on US technology. Jeremy Singer,

00:09:10.673 --> 00:09:15.233
a professor at the University of Glasgow School of Computing Science, said the problem showed

00:09:15.233 --> 00:09:19.773
the dependence built on one company's digital infrastructure and might push people to ask

00:09:19.773 --> 00:09:24.333
whether the information technology ecosystem needs more resilience. Those are arguments

00:09:24.333 --> 00:09:27.593
about what should follow a restoration Microsoft says is already done.

